WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:18.699 --> 00:00:21.039
It was one of the greatest states in history.

00:00:21.039 --> 00:00:25.289
In the early 20th Century, the Austro-Hungarian
Empire dominated central Europe.

00:00:25.289 --> 00:00:30.580
At its height, it stretched all the way from
modern-day Montenegro right up to the Czech-German

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:31.580
border.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:36.149
Ruled for centuries by a succession of Habsburg
emperors, it seemed as permanent as the United

00:00:36.149 --> 00:00:37.550
States does to us today.

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:41.820
Yet, in late 1918, in the blink of an eye,
this great empire collapsed.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:44.899
The man who kicked its foundations out?

00:00:44.899 --> 00:00:50.800
An inexperienced, 31-year old dilettante known
to history as Karl I: the last emperor of

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:51.800
Austria.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:57.170
Before he dynamited his own empire, Karl had
been an obscure royal from a side-branch of

00:00:57.170 --> 00:00:58.170
the Habsburg dynasty.

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:03.120
But a combination of freak tragedies had seen
him propelled to the height of power.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:07.780
Made emperor before he was 30, Karl inherited
an empire on the losing side of one of the

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:09.350
greatest wars in history.

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:14.549
In his short reign, he first tried to hold
his disintegrating world together… before

00:01:14.549 --> 00:01:17.200
finally surrendering to its destruction.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:22.509
An emperor who killed his empire, a soldier
who may become a saint, this is the fascinating

00:01:22.509 --> 00:01:27.240
story of Austria’s last emperor.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:34.420
A Family in Crisis
Fittingly for a man whose reign revolved around

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:36.700
war, Karl’s story started with a bullet.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:39.619
Well, specifically, it started with two bullets.

00:01:39.619 --> 00:01:45.610
Two shots fired in a palatial hunting lodge
outside Vienna one cold, miserable night in

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:46.969
January, 1889.

00:01:46.969 --> 00:01:51.359
Earlier that day, the disturbed young heir
to the Austrian throne - Crown Prince Rudolf

00:01:51.359 --> 00:01:54.799
- had requested his 17-year old lover join
him at the lodge.

00:01:54.799 --> 00:01:59.020
There, the two had made a suicide pact, before
Rudolf shot her and then turned the gun on

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:00.020
himself.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:02.109
The death of the heir shook the empire.

00:02:02.109 --> 00:02:07.560
It created a scandal that engulfed Vienna,
and emotionally broke the emperor, Franz Josef.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:08.880
But it did something else, too.

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:12.170
It set Karl on a collision course with destiny.

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:17.819
Born a year and a half earlier, on August
17, 1887, Karl was a young boy from a side-branch

00:02:17.819 --> 00:02:19.319
of the Habsburg royal family.

00:02:19.319 --> 00:02:24.459
His father, Archduke Otto, was the nephew
of Emperor Franz Josef, and younger brother

00:02:24.459 --> 00:02:26.260
of Archduke Franz Ferdinand.

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:31.290
But while both the Franzes were famous for
their pious, Catholic lifestyles, Karl’s

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:35.220
father was infamous for treating life like
one giant frat party.

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:36.220
He drank.

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:37.220
He caroused.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:39.250
He had affairs that scandalized Austria.

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:42.319
Naturally, this didn’t sit well with his
wife.

00:02:42.319 --> 00:02:46.930
Princess Maria Josepha was a strict Catholic
who couldn’t believe her real-life Disney

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:51.170
prince had turned out to be Gaston minus the
muscles.

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:55.040
From the moment Karl was born, she avoided
her husband in favor of their son, taking

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:56.829
him to mass almost every day.

00:02:56.829 --> 00:03:01.460
It was a habit that would instill in the young
boy a deep affinity for Catholicism.

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:07.280
So that was the family set-up: a not-so abnormal
tale of parental division that could’ve

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:11.519
gone unchanged for decades… had Rudolf not
fired those fatal shots.

00:03:11.519 --> 00:03:12.519
Because Rudolf had been Emperor Franz Josef’s
only son, the line of succession passed to

00:03:12.519 --> 00:03:13.519
Karl’s uncle, Franz Ferdinand.

00:03:13.519 --> 00:03:16.819
Since Ferdinand was childless, that meant
Karl was now third in line to the throne.

00:03:16.819 --> 00:03:17.909
Well, technically.

00:03:17.909 --> 00:03:20.640
Franz Ferdinand was only childless because
he was still young.

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:25.150
It was fully expected that he would marry
and start creating mini-Habsburgs that would

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:27.790
knock Karl back out the running to be emperor.

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:32.480
So no-one made any special effort to start
preparing Karl for life at the top.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:36.739
He was allowed to skip the standard Habsburg
military upbringing, and instead attend a

00:03:36.739 --> 00:03:37.739
regular school.

00:03:37.739 --> 00:03:42.379
But, events would soon conspire to keep Karl’s
place in the succession.

00:03:42.379 --> 00:03:45.739
In 1900, Franz Ferdinand married Sophie Chotek.

00:03:45.739 --> 00:03:51.459
Now, Sophie was a Czech countess, but for
a family as absurdly royal and absurdly Austrian

00:03:51.459 --> 00:03:56.970
as the Habsburgs, “Czech countess” was
practically synonymous with “trailer trash

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:57.970
tramp”.

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:01.599
Enraged by the match, Franz Josef excluded
Franz Ferdinand’s future children from the

00:04:01.599 --> 00:04:02.920
line of succession.

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:07.180
And just like that, it dawned on everyone
that Karl really would be emperor one day.

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:11.209
It was too late to stick Karl on the military
track, so his parents allowed him to go to

00:04:11.209 --> 00:04:14.109
university in Prague, to get a regular education.

00:04:14.109 --> 00:04:19.270
In the grand, Czech city, Karl absorbed ideas
and attitudes alien to his family; becoming

00:04:19.270 --> 00:04:23.139
a cosmopolitan reformist, even as he held
onto his strong Catholicism.

00:04:23.139 --> 00:04:28.070
At first, the emperor must’ve consoled himself
that the throne was still a long way from

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:29.070
the boy’s grasp.

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:31.240
But it soon got closer.

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:37.010
On November 1, 1906, Karl’s father Otto
passed away from syphilis, making Karl second

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:39.730
in line, behind only Franz Ferdinand.

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:46.830
Now all it would take would be a single accident
or, say, a single assassin’s bullet, and

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:49.810
19-year old Karl would be the heir presumptive.

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:55.060
No-one knew it in 1906, but that assassin’s
bullet was already in Franz Ferdinand’s

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:56.060
future.

00:04:56.060 --> 00:05:00.620
And, when it was finally fired, it was going
to change the entire world.

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:07.810
Prelude to Conflict
For the next eight years, Karl suffered a

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:11.960
crash course in the kind of lives Habsburg
heirs were expected to live.

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:16.250
With the young student now getting worryingly
close to power, Franz Josef abandoned his

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:20.450
previous detachment, and worked flat-out to
whip Karl into shape.

00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:24.540
The boy was removed from university, refused
the chance to finish his degree, and dumped

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:29.160
onto an emergency military track that saw
him whisked away to garrisons all over the

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:30.160
empire.

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:34.520
He was also bumped into an arranged marriage
with a bride the emperor thought worthy, the

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:38.120
suitably-wealthy, suitably-royal Zita of Bourbon-Parma.

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:42.350
There’s actually footage of the elderly
Franz Josef at the couple’s wedding, watching

00:05:42.350 --> 00:05:47.320
them closely, like a hawk suspicious its prey
might run off at any moment.

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:48.960
He needn’t have worried.

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:52.830
Despite their arranged marriage, Karl and
Zita got along fine, bonding over their shared

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:55.800
Catholic faith and interest in new ideas.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:59.901
But while Franz Josef may have breathed a
sigh of relief over the wedding, there were

00:05:59.901 --> 00:06:01.830
already signs of trouble.

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:07.370
Although Karl was now having a normal Habsburg
upbringing, no-one was actually showing him

00:06:07.370 --> 00:06:08.740
how to run an empire.

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:13.430
Franz Josef kept him at arms’ length from
court, possibly on the assumption that Karl

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:16.590
could learn later, when Franz Ferdinand was
emperor.

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:17.900
It was a risky decision.

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:21.030
By the time of Karl’s wedding, Franz Josef
was already in his eighties.

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:26.360
His relationship with his heir, Franz Ferdinand,
was still in the sewer from the whole Sophie

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:27.360
Chotek thing.

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:31.680
If Franz Josef wanted to pass any wisdom on,
now was the time to do it.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:35.260
But maybe the emperor was simply too busy
come 1911.

00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:38.400
After all, his empire was creaking at the
seams.

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:43.810
Although it was known as Austria-Hungary,
the empire Karl would soon inherit was far

00:06:43.810 --> 00:06:46.720
bigger than just those two countries.

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:51.690
Alongside Austria and Hungary, it comprised
modern-day Czech Republic, Slovakia, Slovenia,

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:57.460
Croatia, and Bosnia, alongside parts of modern
Montenegro, Italy, Poland, Serbia, Ukraine,

00:06:57.460 --> 00:06:58.460
and Romania.

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:04.310
That meant a multicultural empire filled with
different ethnicities, religions, and languages.

00:07:04.310 --> 00:07:09.280
It was this multiculturalism that had allowed
Vienna to thrive in the 19th Century.

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:12.940
It was also what was now threatning to pull
it apart.

00:07:12.940 --> 00:07:14.640
Nationalism was on the rise across Europe.

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:18.900
Within the empire, the biggest flashpoints
were the succession-minded Czech lands, and

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:21.000
the newly-acquired territory of Bosnia.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:23.020
The latter was especially volatile.

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:28.380
Only annexed by the empire in 1908, its status
as part of Austria-Hungary was a sore spot

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:33.770
for many Bosnian Serbs, who dreamed of joining
Bosnia and Serbia into a single nation.

00:07:33.770 --> 00:07:38.090
But although Franz Josef was aware of the
problems in Bosnia, he seemingly didn’t

00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:42.090
realize just how acute they had become until
it was too late.

00:07:42.090 --> 00:07:47.960
On July 28, 1914, Karl’s uncle, Franz Ferdinand,
visited Sarajevo as part of his tour of Bosnia.

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:51.850
While he was being driven through the city
in an open-topped car, a young Bosnian Serb

00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:56.190
named Gavrilo Princip stepped out the crowd
and fired two shots.

00:07:56.190 --> 00:08:02.390
Just as Rudolf’s two fatal shots had many
decades before, these two shots fired in Sarajevo

00:08:02.390 --> 00:08:03.670
would change history.

00:08:03.670 --> 00:08:07.570
The assassination of Franz Ferdinand meant
Karl became heir presumptive.

00:08:07.570 --> 00:08:13.050
Suddenly, from a minor royal, this boy of
not-yet 25 had been propelled to the very

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:14.310
cusp of power.

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:19.300
But no-one apart from Karl was thinking about
this in mid-1914.

00:08:19.300 --> 00:08:23.330
The assassination of Franz Ferdinand had already
created much bigger problems.

00:08:23.330 --> 00:08:27.580
Convinced the Serbian government had been
behind the murder, Franz Josef declared war.

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:31.860
This led to Russia coming to Serbia’s aid,
which led to Germany coming to Austria-Hungary’s

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:37.960
aid, which led to France coming to Russia’s
aid, and so on and on until the entire continent

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:41.029
was marching to a military drumbeat.

00:08:41.029 --> 00:08:42.919
WWI had just begun.

00:08:42.919 --> 00:08:49.420
The deadliest conflict the human race had
yet seen would spend the next four years ravaging

00:08:49.420 --> 00:08:50.420
Europe.

00:08:50.420 --> 00:08:55.880
By the time it ended, in November 1918, Karl
would be emperor of an empire that no longer

00:08:55.880 --> 00:08:58.210
existed.

00:08:58.210 --> 00:09:04.520
The Soldier
Despite now being heir to the throne, Karl

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:05.860
was still technically a soldier.

00:09:05.860 --> 00:09:09.860
So, when the largest war ever seen broke out,
he did what soldiers do.

00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:11.420
He went off to fight.

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:16.590
Although the Western Front gets all the English
language press, the Eastern Front was no picnic.

00:09:16.590 --> 00:09:21.780
By the time Karl reached Galicia - the little
slice of Ukraine the empire owned - the bloodshed

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:24.010
had already reached staggering heights.

00:09:24.010 --> 00:09:28.500
Along the front, the Russian empire was making
mincemeat of the Austro-Hungarians.

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:32.780
In the first two months of fighting alone,
the Russians managed to push 300km into Austrian

00:09:32.780 --> 00:09:39.030
territory, capturing Lemberg - now known as
Lviv - and killing over 100,000 Austrian soldiers.

00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:43.420
But while the Romanov family was sitting pretty
in St Petersburg, Karl witnessed the slaughter

00:09:43.420 --> 00:09:44.420
firsthand.

00:09:44.420 --> 00:09:46.810
To say it had an effect on the young man would
be an understatement.

00:09:46.810 --> 00:09:52.330
We’ve read sources say he tried praying
on the battlefield to make the bloodshed stop,

00:09:52.330 --> 00:09:55.600
but if God was up there, he wasn’t in the
mood for listening.

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:59.550
From September to May, the Russians battered
the Austro-Hungarian army.

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:03.340
As they did so, a few things became painfully
clear to Karl.

00:10:03.340 --> 00:10:08.390
The first was that the army was weak not just
because it was in urgent need of modernization,

00:10:08.390 --> 00:10:12.740
but because there was something rotten in
Austria-Hungary itself.

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:17.680
The siren song of nationalism was fraying
the empire’s bonds, making its different

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:21.500
ethnicities mistrustful, unwilling to fight
as a team.

00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:27.320
The second was that the main threat to the
army might not be marauding Russians, but

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:29.670
getting swallowed by the German war machine.

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:34.990
During the Gorlice–Tarnów Offensive in
mid-1915, Karl saw firsthand how it was only

00:10:34.990 --> 00:10:38.450
with German firepower that the empire was
capable of fighting on.

00:10:38.450 --> 00:10:44.090
Over the next 18 months, Karl toured the various
fronts of WWI, noting with increasing alarm

00:10:44.090 --> 00:10:46.029
the empire’s growing problems.

00:10:46.029 --> 00:10:51.640
In northern Italy, he saw how ready to throw
off the Habsburg yoke his subjects were.

00:10:51.640 --> 00:10:56.020
On the Eastern Front, he watched as Russian
manpower smashed the Austro-Hungarian armies

00:10:56.020 --> 00:10:59.140
until their morale was non-existent.

00:10:59.140 --> 00:11:04.180
By late 1916, it was badly clear that - even
if the Central Powers won - the stress of

00:11:04.180 --> 00:11:07.150
fighting might kill the empire stone dead.

00:11:07.150 --> 00:11:10.240
What was desperately needed was someone new
in charge.

00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:14.220
Someone with a vision for how to stop the
slaughter, and pacify the empire’s many

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:15.470
ethnic groups.

00:11:15.470 --> 00:11:18.520
Naturally, Karl assumed he should be that
someone.

00:11:18.520 --> 00:11:20.930
And he was about to get his chance.

00:11:20.930 --> 00:11:26.470
In the early hours of November 21, 1916, emperor
Franz Josef passed away in his bedchamber

00:11:26.470 --> 00:11:30.340
in Vienna, after ruling the empire for nearly
68 years.

00:11:30.340 --> 00:11:34.120
Immediately, Karl was recalled from the front,
arriving in Vienna shortly after.

00:11:34.120 --> 00:11:39.740
There, amid the cold grandeur of the imperial
city, he was proclaimed Karl I: emperor of

00:11:39.740 --> 00:11:40.740
Austria.

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:44.270
At the moment he took the title, he was just
29 years old.

00:11:44.270 --> 00:11:48.410
From the go, Karl was determined to act on
the lessons he’d learned on the front.

00:11:48.410 --> 00:11:51.120
That meant ending the war, and ending it soon.

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:55.550
He even insisted on having the following phrase
inserted into his coronation proclamation:

00:11:55.550 --> 00:12:01.560
“I will do everything to banish in the shortest
possible time the horrors and sacrifices of

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:06.600
war, to win back for my peoples the sorely-missed
blessings of peace.”

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:11.580
But despite the new emperor’s grand dreams,
the reality was that peace wasn’t his to

00:12:11.580 --> 00:12:12.580
give.

00:12:12.580 --> 00:12:16.560
By winter 1916, Austria-Hungary was completely
reliant on Germany.

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:20.900
And the chances of the Kaiser agreeing to
peace were somewhere around the chances of

00:12:20.900 --> 00:12:26.310
the Kaiser agreeing to put on heels and dance
for Karl in a Berlin nightclub.

00:12:26.310 --> 00:12:28.910
But Karl was not to be deterred.

00:12:28.910 --> 00:12:34.089
Even as he was hastily arranging his separate
coronation in the Hungarian half of the empire,

00:12:34.089 --> 00:12:37.240
the young emperor was coming up with a dangerous
plan.

00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:39.700
The Kaiser wouldn’t let Karl pursue peace?

00:12:39.700 --> 00:13:50.820
Then Karl was just gonna have to go over the
old man’s head to get it.

00:13:50.820 --> 00:14:02.350
The Emperor
If Karl

00:14:02.350 --> 00:14:06.880
wanted his empire to make peace with the Allies,
the first thing he had to do was make sure

00:14:06.880 --> 00:14:08.480
it survived long enough.

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:12.120
The disease of nationalism had by now spread
to Vienna itself.

00:14:12.120 --> 00:14:16.730
Fearing the divisions if ultra-right Austrians
were given too much control, Karl made one

00:14:16.730 --> 00:14:20.420
of his first acts hiring prominent Czechs
into his cabinet.

00:14:20.420 --> 00:14:23.970
It was a move meant to neutralize the threat
of Czech secession.

00:14:23.970 --> 00:14:29.330
Instead, it just meant Karl became as unpopular
with the Austrians as he was with everyone

00:14:29.330 --> 00:14:30.330
else.

00:14:30.330 --> 00:14:34.990
And, unpopular was exactly the last thing
Karl wanted to be right now.

00:14:34.990 --> 00:14:40.510
That March, 1917, the February Revolution
shook Russia, toppling the Tsar.

00:14:40.510 --> 00:14:43.760
And, yes, we agree it’s confusing that a
revolution named after the month of February

00:14:43.760 --> 00:14:45.480
took place in March.

00:14:45.480 --> 00:14:49.160
Blame Imperial Russia’s weird love affair
with the Julian Calendar.

00:14:49.160 --> 00:14:54.080
Anyway, the Wrong Month Revolution put the
fear of God into Karl, who started to worry

00:14:54.080 --> 00:14:57.540
that he might be next if he didn’t get this
peace thing sorted.

00:14:57.540 --> 00:15:03.180
So he got his wife Zita to contact her brothers,
Sixtus and Xavier, who were fighting on the

00:15:03.180 --> 00:15:04.340
Allied side.

00:15:04.340 --> 00:15:08.680
He then gave them a secret note to hand to
the French, telling them Austria-Hungary wanted

00:15:08.680 --> 00:15:09.680
to make peace.

00:15:09.680 --> 00:15:14.880
Today, the Sixtus Affair is infamous for making
Karl look like a jackass.

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:21.500
But, in March and April of 1917, it briefly
looked like the key to ending the war.

00:15:21.500 --> 00:15:26.410
When the French received Karl’s overtures,
they laid out a list of cautious demands as

00:15:26.410 --> 00:15:28.340
a precondition for talks.

00:15:28.340 --> 00:15:32.480
When Karl immediately agreed to most of them,
the French contacted London, being all like

00:15:32.480 --> 00:15:35.010
“whoa... this guy’s actually serious!”

00:15:35.010 --> 00:15:38.940
Everyone was so excited by the messages that
British Prime Minister Lloyd George remarked

00:15:38.940 --> 00:15:40.190
“that means peace.”

00:15:40.190 --> 00:15:43.630
But, of course, Lloyd George was wrong.

00:15:43.630 --> 00:15:48.250
While Karl was happy to leave Germany hanging,
willing to return land to Serbia, and willing

00:15:48.250 --> 00:15:52.560
to recognize French claims to new territory,
he just couldn’t bring himself to give up

00:15:52.560 --> 00:15:55.670
the Habsburgs’ last remaining chunks of
Italy.

00:15:55.670 --> 00:15:59.910
Since Italy was demanding he do just that
in return for peace, the secret negotiations

00:15:59.910 --> 00:16:01.100
eventually fizzled.

00:16:01.100 --> 00:16:04.520
With hindsight, Karl’s actions here were
super dumb.

00:16:04.520 --> 00:16:07.090
Austro-Hungary’s holdings in Italy were
already small.

00:16:07.090 --> 00:16:10.830
While abandoning them would’ve been a bitter
pill to swallow, it could’ve allowed the

00:16:10.830 --> 00:16:11.830
empire to survive.

00:16:11.830 --> 00:16:14.060
But that was just one of Karl’s contradictions.

00:16:14.060 --> 00:16:18.810
He was a wannabee peacemaker, but he was also
a guy who literally thought the Catholic God

00:16:18.810 --> 00:16:21.560
had appointed him to rule an indivisible empire.

00:16:21.560 --> 00:16:25.630
With that mindset, surrendering territory
was always gonna be a non-starter.

00:16:25.630 --> 00:16:29.730
But even though Karl’s negotiations fell
through, it wasn’t the end of the Sixtus

00:16:29.730 --> 00:16:30.730
Affair.

00:16:30.730 --> 00:16:35.649
On January 8, 1918, Woodrow Wilson set out
his 14 points for peace in Europe, including

00:16:35.649 --> 00:16:41.680
self-determination for all peoples of Karl’s
empire - a move that almost guaranteed its

00:16:41.680 --> 00:16:42.680
dissolution.

00:16:42.680 --> 00:16:46.330
When Karl subsequently tried to strike a more
strident tone against the Allies, the French

00:16:46.330 --> 00:16:48.339
published his secret letters.

00:16:48.339 --> 00:16:54.230
This is the bit of the Sixtus Affair everyone
remembers, and it was embarrassing as it sounds.

00:16:54.230 --> 00:16:58.370
For the Allies, this was final proof that
Austria-Hungary was close to collapse.

00:16:58.370 --> 00:17:01.000
For Germany, it was nothing short of betrayal.

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:03.120
That May, the Kaiser angrily summoned Karl.

00:17:03.120 --> 00:17:08.260
In a special meeting, he forced the young
emperor to put the Austro-Hungarian military

00:17:08.260 --> 00:17:10.140
totally under Berlin’s control.

00:17:10.140 --> 00:17:14.179
With the empire now little more than a German
vassal state, the writing was on the wall.

00:17:14.179 --> 00:17:19.130
There was no way Karl was escaping this quagmire
without some serious consequences.

00:17:19.130 --> 00:17:24.139
But not even the failed emperor could’ve
forseen just how serious those consequences

00:17:24.139 --> 00:17:27.880
would be.

00:17:27.880 --> 00:17:33.909
Collapse
Across 1918, Karl tried his best to shore

00:17:33.909 --> 00:17:36.480
up support inside his ailing empire.

00:17:36.480 --> 00:17:38.239
He introduced military reforms.

00:17:38.239 --> 00:17:39.429
Banned flogging.

00:17:39.429 --> 00:17:41.320
Tried to halt the use of poison gas.

00:17:41.320 --> 00:17:45.999
On the domestic front, he set up a welfare
system for returning soldiers, tried to demilitarize

00:17:45.999 --> 00:17:46.999
his society.

00:17:46.999 --> 00:17:49.369
But it was too little, too late.

00:17:49.369 --> 00:17:53.240
When Austria-Hungary entered the war, it was
not a given that it would collapse.

00:17:53.240 --> 00:17:58.450
Had Karl’s peace efforts succeeded in 1917,
it seems likely some form of the empire would’ve

00:17:58.450 --> 00:18:03.830
survived - minus its Italian, Serbian, and
probably Polish and Czech parts, but still

00:18:03.830 --> 00:18:04.830
an empire.

00:18:04.830 --> 00:18:09.090
By fall, 1918, though, that ship had long
sailed.

00:18:09.090 --> 00:18:14.520
On October 16, Karl published a manifesto
broadly accepting Wilson’s 14 Points, declared

00:18:14.520 --> 00:18:19.470
the creation of a federal empire, and granted
every nationality autonomy.

00:18:19.470 --> 00:18:21.129
But it was all just theatre.

00:18:21.129 --> 00:18:23.840
By now, it was clear WWI was in its endgame.

00:18:23.840 --> 00:18:27.239
And it was clear who was on the losing side.

00:18:27.239 --> 00:18:32.149
Just 12 days after Karl published his manifesto,
on October 28, the Czechs took control of

00:18:32.149 --> 00:18:33.929
Prague and declared their independence.

00:18:33.929 --> 00:18:38.280
The very next day, Croatia and Slovenia unilaterally
seceded.

00:18:38.280 --> 00:18:40.619
The day after that, Slovakia jumped ship.

00:18:40.619 --> 00:18:45.840
That same day, Austria’s politicians declared
the creation of a new, national parliament,

00:18:45.840 --> 00:18:48.460
effectively taking Austria out of its own
empire.

00:18:48.460 --> 00:18:52.830
Within 24 hours, Hungary’s new leaders had
dissolved the treaty joining their country

00:18:52.830 --> 00:18:53.830
with Austria.

00:18:53.830 --> 00:18:59.049
On November 11, with only a handful of fragments
of his empire left, Karl gave up trying to

00:18:59.049 --> 00:19:00.049
hold on.

00:19:00.049 --> 00:19:04.679
He renounced his right to rule Austria, released
his subjects from their oath of loyalty, and

00:19:04.679 --> 00:19:06.700
announced he would retreat from politics.

00:19:06.700 --> 00:19:10.679
It wasn’t quite an abdication, but it was
very close.

00:19:10.679 --> 00:19:15.980
As the guns fell silent along the front at
11am that same day, Austria-Hungary officially

00:19:15.980 --> 00:19:17.340
ceased to exist.

00:19:17.340 --> 00:19:19.570
It seems Karl still had hope at this point.

00:19:19.570 --> 00:19:24.120
Hope his grateful subjects would recall him
for his peace efforts, and ask him to lead

00:19:24.120 --> 00:19:25.299
them once again.

00:19:25.299 --> 00:19:29.450
It was a nice fantasy, but a fantasy was all
it was.

00:19:29.450 --> 00:19:33.529
While some call Karl the “Peace Emperor”
today, the fact is that he totally failed

00:19:33.529 --> 00:19:35.280
in his goal of making peace.

00:19:35.280 --> 00:19:37.299
WWI had just shattered Europe.

00:19:37.299 --> 00:19:40.009
It had claimed over 20 million lives.

00:19:40.009 --> 00:19:44.799
No-one but no-one wanted to be led by one
of the guys on its losing side.

00:19:44.799 --> 00:19:47.929
Come March, 1919, even Karl seemed to recognize
this.

00:19:47.929 --> 00:19:52.830
At the end of that month, he and Zita boarded
a train bound for Switzerland, and set off

00:19:52.830 --> 00:19:54.860
for a lonely life as exiles.

00:19:54.860 --> 00:19:58.529
In the wake of their departure, the Austrian
national parliament passed a law banning all

00:19:58.529 --> 00:20:02.559
Habsburgs from the country, and stripping
them of their right to the throne.

00:20:02.559 --> 00:20:04.989
Any sensible person would’ve seen that this
was the end.

00:20:04.989 --> 00:20:08.450
That the video was coming to its close, that
the credits on Karl’s life were about to

00:20:08.450 --> 00:20:09.559
roll.

00:20:09.559 --> 00:20:11.899
But Karl wasn’t sensible.

00:20:11.899 --> 00:20:16.350
He was an idealist, and a man who thought
God had handpicked him for greatness.

00:20:16.350 --> 00:20:21.049
As he crossed the Swiss border, the deposed
emperor issued his Feldkirch Manifesto, revoking

00:20:21.049 --> 00:20:26.710
his own decision to step down, and announcing
he would return to lead Austria-Hungary again.

00:20:26.710 --> 00:20:29.549
It was a moment as brazen as it was foolish.

00:20:29.549 --> 00:20:35.620
It was also a moment that would soon plunge
the shattered empire right back into chaos.

00:20:35.620 --> 00:20:43.879
You Can Never Go Back
Although with hindsight we can see that Karl’s

00:20:43.879 --> 00:20:47.400
reign was over, things weren’t so certain
in 1919.

00:20:47.400 --> 00:20:51.830
France actually toyed with restoring him and
rebuilding his empire as a counterweight to

00:20:51.830 --> 00:20:52.830
Germany.

00:20:52.830 --> 00:20:56.519
It was just spitballing, but word somehow
got back to Karl, whose takeaway was that

00:20:56.519 --> 00:20:59.450
Paris would support him retaking his throne
by force.

00:20:59.450 --> 00:21:02.409
The only problem was, there was no throne
for him to go back to.

00:21:02.409 --> 00:21:03.879
Or was there?

00:21:03.879 --> 00:21:08.080
In WWI’s aftermath, Hungary very quickly
went through a phase of Communist rule, which

00:21:08.080 --> 00:21:13.950
ended with everyone really, super keen to
do the opposite of Communism.

00:21:13.950 --> 00:21:18.519
In February, 1920, Miklós Horthy came to
power, restored the monarchy, and named himself

00:21:18.519 --> 00:21:20.250
regent in Karl’s name.

00:21:20.250 --> 00:21:24.309
For Karl, this was a clear signal that the
Hungarians, at least, wanted him back; and

00:21:24.309 --> 00:21:26.260
he wasn’t totally mistaken.

00:21:26.260 --> 00:21:30.840
But there was one Hungarian determined to
make sure Karl never retook his throne.

00:21:30.840 --> 00:21:34.299
Horthy may have restored the monarchy, but
only as a means to power.

00:21:34.299 --> 00:21:37.840
After all, what’s a regent when his king
is capable of ruling?

00:21:37.840 --> 00:21:39.169
A nobody.

00:21:39.169 --> 00:21:44.559
So when Karl snuck into Hungary in disguise
on March 26, 1921, Horthy did everything in

00:21:44.559 --> 00:21:47.000
his power to keep the young man from unseating
him.

00:21:47.000 --> 00:21:51.370
When they met, he told Karl that of course
he supported him, that of course he’d help

00:21:51.370 --> 00:21:52.519
him march on Budapest.

00:21:52.519 --> 00:21:58.200
Then, when the emperor was good and mollified,
Horthy snuck off and asked the French if they

00:21:58.200 --> 00:22:00.650
were, like, actually serious about supporting
Karl.

00:22:00.650 --> 00:22:04.399
To Horthy’s delight, the French were not
serious about it at all.

00:22:04.399 --> 00:22:09.289
Paris announced they’d had no part of this,
that Karl was both an idiot and on his own.

00:22:09.289 --> 00:22:13.200
This delighted Prague, and the Czechoslovaks
immediately threatened to declare war on Hungary

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:14.809
if it restored the emperor.

00:22:14.809 --> 00:22:20.039
And that’s how Karl wound up fleeing into
exile in Switzerland for the second time.

00:22:20.039 --> 00:22:23.470
But the last emperor wasn’t done trying
yet.

00:22:23.470 --> 00:22:27.749
That fall, Karl contacted Hungary’s elites,
making sure to exclude Horthy.

00:22:27.749 --> 00:22:30.740
He managed to drum up an army of 5,000 loyalists.

00:22:30.740 --> 00:22:35.480
On October 20, the emperor flew a flight plane
to the border town of Sopron, where his army

00:22:35.480 --> 00:22:36.629
was waiting for him.

00:22:36.629 --> 00:22:40.309
Initially the venture had all the hallmarks
of a glorious homecoming.

00:22:40.309 --> 00:22:42.769
The people of Sopron celebrated Karl’s return.

00:22:42.769 --> 00:22:46.679
As his army began the long march to Budapest,
more and more joined their ranks.

00:22:46.679 --> 00:22:51.399
For one shining moment, it really looked like
Karl was going to get his empire back.

00:22:51.399 --> 00:22:55.100
And then the army reached Budapest and everything
fell apart.

00:22:55.100 --> 00:22:58.590
No-one seems quite sure what happened that
strange day.

00:22:58.590 --> 00:23:04.039
We’ve seen sources claim Horthy tricked
the citizens into taking up arms against Karl,

00:23:04.039 --> 00:23:07.320
believing he was at the head of a Czechoslovak
invasion force.

00:23:07.320 --> 00:23:11.239
We’ve seen others say Karl was betrayed
by one of his generals.

00:23:11.239 --> 00:23:15.520
The only thing certain is that Karl entered
Budapest on October 23.

00:23:15.520 --> 00:23:20.980
By October 24, he’d been captured, and his
last chance at restoration was over.

00:23:20.980 --> 00:23:24.929
This time, it wouldn’t end with comfy exile
in Switzerland.

00:23:24.929 --> 00:23:29.799
On November 1, the irate British sailed a
warship up the Danube, and demanded Karl be

00:23:29.799 --> 00:23:30.799
handed over.

00:23:30.799 --> 00:23:35.679
They then took him away - away from Hungary,
away from mainland Europe - away to the Portuguese

00:23:35.679 --> 00:23:39.769
island of Madeira, some 900km from the mainland.

00:23:39.769 --> 00:23:40.769
And they left him there.

00:23:40.769 --> 00:23:41.769
And that was it for Austria’s last emperor.

00:23:41.769 --> 00:23:47.289
On April 1, 1922, Karl succumbed to pneumonia
- some sources say Spanish flu - and passed

00:23:47.289 --> 00:23:49.009
away, aged just 34.

00:23:49.009 --> 00:23:52.309
At the time he died, his name was mud across
Europe.

00:23:52.309 --> 00:23:55.559
His former nations had all passed laws banning
him from their territory.

00:23:55.559 --> 00:24:00.399
He was seen as a weakling at best, a laughingstock
at worst.

00:24:00.399 --> 00:24:02.450
But things soon started to change.

00:24:02.450 --> 00:24:07.940
In the aftermath of WWII, with its overtly
evil trio of Hitler, Mussolini, and Stalin,

00:24:07.940 --> 00:24:13.460
a bumbling Habsburg who’d sincerely tried
to make peace suddenly didn’t look so bad.

00:24:13.460 --> 00:24:19.229
As WWI began to be viewed as a gigantic, needless,
morally gray screw-up, Karl’s determination

00:24:19.229 --> 00:24:22.950
to end the war eventually began to appear
decent.

00:24:22.950 --> 00:24:24.399
Even noble.

00:24:24.399 --> 00:24:30.159
In 2004, over 80 years after Austria’s last
emperor died in misery, Pope John Paul II

00:24:30.159 --> 00:24:32.669
beatified him for his peacemaking efforts.

00:24:32.669 --> 00:24:36.830
As of the time of writing, Karl is on the
path to sainthood.

00:24:36.830 --> 00:24:38.330
Is it the fate he deserves?

00:24:38.330 --> 00:24:39.610
Who can say.

00:24:39.610 --> 00:24:46.009
While Karl really did try to make peace, he
also really was an ineffectual bumbler, who

00:24:46.009 --> 00:24:49.100
failed to end the war his empire was part
of.

00:24:49.100 --> 00:24:54.330
They say that all it takes for evil to triumph
is for good men to do nothing.

00:24:54.330 --> 00:24:59.889
What they neglect to mention is that - sometimes
- good men really can try to do something,

00:24:59.889 --> 00:25:02.590
and still fail to stop the slaughter.

00:25:02.590 --> 00:25:05.580
Karl may have been a decent man deep down.

00:25:05.580 --> 00:25:10.159
But if his story shows us anything, it’s
that decency isn’t always enough.

00:25:10.159 --> 00:25:16.210
Sometimes, you can do everything in your power
to hold your country together, and still wind

00:25:16.210 --> 00:25:33.570
up being known to history as 
the last emperor.

